## 8. Hair that acts like hair: lock cutting (2026-10-18, AUTO tools + AI config)
**Diagnosis rule.** A hair mesh hung on a few chains with blocky weights moves as one block. Shion's main sheet had
1 108 vertices on 3 chains of 6 bones, with 39 % of the hair vertices rigid to one bone, and its UV-split layers tore
5.6x in a bend test. To check a new model, look at chains per hanging sheet, the rigid share and the median max weight.
**How the developer did it** **[obs: 18 creator source models vs shipped]**
* The creators' FBX already contain dense hair chains (Queen 52 hair bones, Asuna 5 back chains x 8, Primrose 67), with
  no `_end` leaves. The developer used them unchanged as cloth roots.
* Shipped survey, 24 characters (`scripts/tfa_bonecloth_survey.py` -> `{SHIPPED_LIB~0}/_bonecloth_survey/`, local):
  * long back hair: 3-12 chains x 8-12 bones, mostly mesh connection (asuna, irelia, power, marin, succubus,
    tifa B_HairC);
  * smooth weights: median max weight 0.45-0.65, rigid share about 0;
  * front and side hair: line chains of 2-6 bones.
**Parameter facts** **[obs: profiles + decomp]**
* 41 of the 44 shipped hair cloths leave `useClampRotation` off. queen B_HairFront and B_HairL are the standard
  front-hair preset (restoreRotation 0.213 -> 0.051, as on power, marin and artoria B_SideBang) plus
  `useClampRotation 1` at 0 deg, a position clamp and triangle bend.
* `ClampRotationJob` (decomp `MagicaCloth_ClampRotationConstraint_ClampRotationJob__Execute`) clamps each non-fixed
  child's direction to within `CurveParam(depth)` deg of `parentRot * localPos`, so 0 deg makes the locks rigid. Never
  copy that switch for hair that should move.
* Most front hair has `useFixedNonRotation 0` (31 of 44).
**Cutter** `scripts/own_hair_locks.py <cfg>` (cfg = AI work; example `shion_build/own/bonecloth/hair_locks_cfg.json`):
* Regions are chosen by the largest component or a dominant-bone regex. Split modes:
  * `fan`: angle around the apex fitted from the sheet's edge lines, with one global normalization. Use it for a sheet
    whose bottom ends in tongues; `lateral` (x divided by local half-width) sent V-bottom tips to non-adjacent locks
    and tore them 16x.
  * `lateral`, `per_component`, `single`.
* Weights are computed per welded position (no seam cracks):
  * the creator's head weight is kept;
  * the remainder goes onto the lock chain with a triangle kernel (`spread` 1.5 bones), using the vertex's closest point
    on the smoothed lock centreline, so both layers of a double sheet share the parameter (no layer shear);
  * lateral blend `blend` at lock borders;
  * top 4 influences, exact uint16 sums.
* The root sits where the mean head weight falls to `root_head_weight`. Bones are `HL_<lock>_<k>` plus `_tip` under
  `parent_bone`.
* Outputs `locks.json` and `<mesh>.npz`. The npz BONES list keeps the build's list as its prefix.
* `--clearance`: per particle, the skin distance, the head-collider distance and the inward thickness, as radius inputs.
**Cloths** (`own_bonecloth.py` plan keys `locks_json`, `lock_regions`, `sheet_links`, `tips: false`):
* `sheet_links` adds struct lines between particle j of neighbouring locks, standing in for mesh connection.
  * Without links, neighbours swinging opposite ways stretch the mesh up to 11.7x; with a coherent swing, 2.96x.
  * When links exist, `create_cloth_data(parent_of=hierarchy)` keeps hierarchy parents. Without that, 13 particles took
    a lateral parent and the line-rotation blocks broke.
* Set `useTriangleBend 0` on line sheets: there are no triangles, and the builder raises otherwise.
* Radius: keep it below each particle's rest distance to its colliders, so nothing starts inside. Shion's head sphere
  sits 4-14 mm inside the forehead, which gives the bangs 10.5 mm.
**Assembler hooks** `own_hair_locks.add_bones(locks_dir, P=, spath=, w_rest=)` right after the skeleton, then
`own_hair_locks.reskin(m, locks_dir, name, known_bones=SPATH)` right after `MeshBuilder.build`:
* `reskin` raises if the build's vertex order differs from the cut.
* The lock bones stay out of skeleton.json, so Tpose, Face_Pose and the Rig lists are unchanged. The shipped Tpose is a
  humanoid muscle clip without hair, and `FaceRig.Init` re-collects every head child into faceBonesL/R/M at runtime
  (`getChildBones`, no filter).
* End-to-end check without the game: `scripts/verify_bonecloth_integration.py`. Shion: ALL_OK, 121 lock bones, rest
  skinning error 8e-8 m, ClothData regenerated from the written bundle equal (`shion_build/proof/hair/`).
**Clipping bound without the game.** Measure the skin under and near the hair against the cloth's colliders: gap = how
far the skin lies outside the colliders, and a particle of radius r sinks at most (gap - r). Shion: back of the head 0,
bangs at most 1.5 mm, shoulder tops at most 6.5 mm (28 vertices, no standard shoulder collider);
`shion_build/proof/hair/head_collider_coverage.json`. Apply the same radius rule to garment tips (jacket 10 -> 4 mm,
cuffs 10 -> 5 mm, so they no longer start inside the ass and forearm colliders).
**In-game hair proof.** `scripts/ingame_test/hair_motion_capture.py` plays the same animated scenes for each actor and
records the head from the back, side and front, with mp4s and contact sheets. Do not judge hair with the harness
`rotate`: it applies the whole yaw in one frame, a step over `teleportRotation` (45 deg) resets the cloth, and the part
above `maxRotationSpeed` (720 deg/s) is ignored (`WorldInfluence.rotationIgnoreRatio`), so the swing is damped and
unrealistic. Animation is what the user sees.
**Pieces the artist painted (2026-10-19, user: "the huge hair pieces are separated; cut them").** A stylised hair sheet can
be one connected mesh (Shion: 1 899 vertices, one source island) whose texture paints separate pieces (7 dark panels with
light borders on the inner side). Rigged as one surface it reads as one plate with drawn lines.
* `scripts/own_hair_panels.py` finds the pieces as texture components inside one UV island (masked by the island's own
  triangles, since the background is dark too).
* It gives every sheet vertex a distance to each piece: by texture on that island, and by the shared back-view
  projection for the other layer (a 3D closest point breaks where the layers diverge).
* It splits the triangles on the medial line d_k = d_k+1 (marching triangles, every attribute interpolated) and
  duplicates the vertices there, except where the creator's head weight is >= 0.9, so the scalp cap stays whole.
* `own_hair_locks.py` mode "panel" then gives one chain per piece. Its hang distance must use the UNCUT surface:
  otherwise a corner cut off from the cap gets s = 1, folds the centreline back up and skins top vertices to the tip
  bone (35x stretch).
* Shion result: 7 panels, max edge stretch 2.8 vs 10.8 for the creator's rig. The assembler swaps the whole mesh:
  `reskin` sees `V_SRC` and checks the build equals the mesh that was cut.
**Hair clipping toolchain (AUTO).**
* `hair_clip_check.py`: hair vertices inside the body and garments, at rest or in harness `diag` dumps (the game's own
  hair bakes vs the CPU-skinned body).
* `own_hair_colliders.py`: hair-only colliders, children of the standard slots, covering the zones the garment-fit
  colliders leave outside:
  * capsules along a slot axis;
  * least-squares spheres for mounds;
  * deep capsules or k-means sphere covers for patches such as the shoulders.
  Listed by hair cloths only.
* `hair_pose_gate.py`: drapes every hair cloth in real game poses with its colliders and counts skin penetration, plus
  the rest push (particles that start inside a collider pop at spawn).
* `hair_solve.py`: grid over particle-radius scale and collider allowance, and drops any hair collider that a cloth's
  styled rest shape already sits inside.
* Shion m21pre in game, before v3: back hair 10-99 vertices inside, up to 30 mm; long strands 29-98, up to 30 mm.
**Capture lesson.** The harness `pose` re-poses whatever actor is loaded: a scene's Human(actorA, name) then shows the
previous character for every name. Use `scene` (a full load, fresh actors) and check `target <name>` in the log.
A stem copied into Mods after game start cannot be loaded without a restart.

## 9. Hair collision solve: per-cloth collider copies and solve parameters (2026-10-19) [obs: offline gate on game-pose dumps]

Order for any long-hair model:
1. Cut the hair (section 8).
2. Fit hair-only colliders.
3. Solve the radius x allowance grid.
4. Gate offline.
5. Deliver.
6. Check in game.

The cookbook script is `shion_build/resume/hair_resolve.sh <tag>` (about 12 min, `lowprio --mem 8`).

**Hair-only colliders** (`scripts/own_hair_colliders.py`, cfg example `shion_build/own/bonecloth/hair_colliders_cfg_m21d2.json`):
- They are children of the standard `ClothColliders/<slot>` transforms. Rig.Init re-parents the slots and carries the
  children along, so there are no new rig bones.
- Fit them to the *body the build ships*: shape keys held at a value, such as BreastRest at 100, must be baked into the
  body npz first. A fit on the old body leaves the strands 2-7 mm inside the grown breast.
- Shape rules:
  - Limbs and neck: a capsule along the slot axis (quantile 0.97, allow 2-3 mm).
  - Chest: a deep capsule (`depth_mm`), so it does not bulge in front of the sternum.
  - Breasts and shoulders: **k-means sphere covers** (`shape: "spheres"`, count 5, depth 25 mm, allow 3 mm).
  - One least-squares sphere per breast bulged up to 34 mm at the cone's lateral slope, which caused a 33.7 mm spawn
    push. Five spheres brought it to 24 mm; a tighter cover is still open.

**Per-cloth collider copy rule (tailoring, `hair_solve.drop_rest_colliders`):**
- If a cloth's styled rest particles (radius x scale) already start inside a hair collider deeper than `--pop-mm`, that
  cloth gets its own copy `<name>__<cloth>_CC` with the radius reduced by the deficit. Other cloths keep the full
  collider. Dropping the collider or shrinking it for every cloth both measured worse.
- **Exception, `--no-tailor <cloth>:<collider substring>`:** use it where the *body grew under the styled hair* (breast
  shape keys). There the push-out at spawn is correct: the hair must move off the new breast.
- **Partial tailoring, `--no-tailor <cloth>:<substring>@<mm>`:** the copy shrinks only by (deficit - mm), so at most
  mm of spawn push remains. This is the middle ground between full tailoring (strands stay inside the grown breast) and
  none (24 mm float).
- Check the size of that push with `_rest_push_detail` in the gate's rest state ([particle, start depth mm, collider];
  `hair_pose_gate.py` without `--diag`). Over about 8 mm the hair visibly floats; fix it with a smaller tip radius or a
  tighter cover.

**Solve parameters that won (Shion, 6 cloths, 13 game-pose states):**
- radius scale 1.25 x the donor curve; collider allowance 2.0 x the fit; 2 chains per painted back panel with links
  only inside a panel (`sheet_links: "within_group"`, split `per_panel: 2`).
- Grid score is lexicographic (rest push > pop_mm, inside total, deepest, rest push).

| Setup | Doggy result |
|---|---|
| 1 chain per panel | 30, up to 16 mm |
| 2 chains per panel | 18, up to 10.4 mm |

Remaining for a head-down pose: back panels enter the face. Options are a head-front capsule for B_HairBack only, or
more bones per panel.

**Clip check in game:**
- The diag dumps hold exact hair vertices (`diag_bake_FBX__<mesh>.bin`, world space).
- The body bake (`Body_`, plus `Body_Breasts` and `Body_Ass` for the cloth jiggle) has the *assembled* vertex count:
  90489 in game vs 70209 in the npz. It cannot pair with the npz triangles.
- `scripts/bundle_mesh_tris.py <tfa> <cache> Body_ Body_Breasts Body_Ass Outfit_Full` caches the bundle's own triangles
  per md5.
  - It reads the raw Mesh bytes: find the m_VertexData header (vc, 14 channels, pos Float32x3), then the int32 length
    that ends there. The typetree of a 90k-vertex mesh with shapes raises MemoryError under 8 GB.
  - With two renderers of one name, the SMR under `.../FBX/` wins.
- `hair_clip_check.py --bundle <installed tfa> --garment-mesh Jacket=Outfit_Full` uses those surfaces. On the m21a dump
  D7_A it was identical to the CPU path, so it is validated.
- `hair_motion_capture.keep_diag` keeps these bakes (`KEEP_BAKES`).

**Capture validity:**
- Use the `scene` command per actor, check the target (`INVALID.txt` when the log shows NOT found), and write
  `installed.txt` (md5).
- Never use `pose` to switch actors: it reuses the loaded actor.
- A stem copied into Mods after the game started cannot load.
- Long diag names need the `\?\` prefix (MAX_PATH).


## 10. Soft-tissue patch cloth (M_Breasts / M_Mouth / M_Ass / M_Throat): budget and error-driven reduction (2026-10-25) [obs: offline on the m21d inputs; in-game A/B pending]

Why: perf gate QA 146 (frame time equal or better than the best shipped character). cost_model 3.3: m21d patches were x2.3 particles, x3.8 constraints, x7 bone entries of tifa.
Recipe (all AUTO, `shion_build/own/bonecloth/patches/`; reasoning and numbers in `PATCH_BUDGET.md`):
1. **Drop dead constraint lists.** The solver runs a bend/near list only if the profile enables it (`RestoreDistanceGroupData.IsValid`: type 1 = `useBend`, type 2 = `useNear`;
   `RestoreDistanceConstraint_DistanceJob__Execute` returns otherwise). The game's M_Breasts/M_Ass/M_Throat profiles have `useBendDistance 0`; only M_Mouth uses bend.
   `own_cloth.build(prune_dead=True)` (default) reads `data/cloth_param_profiles.json` (`profile_flags`) and builds only enabled kinds: Shion 118 374 -> 55 754 + 7 148 constraints, no change in look or motion.
2. **List only the bones a patch weights** (`own_cloth.build(compact=True)`, cfg `cloth_compact_bones`; assembler edits by `patches/apply_cloth_compact.py`): the virtual
   MeshData, patch Mesh, patch SMR `m_Bones` and `MagicaVirtualDeformer.boneList` (shipped 13-55 bones each; ours listed the whole skeleton): Shion 668 -> 58 bone entries.
3. **Reduce particles by error, not by one length.** A global `reduce_mm` merges vertices whose shape deltas differ (Vagina key up to 35 mm, InnerMesh 40, Mask_* collapse
   shapes): the merged particle carries the MEAN delta, so the render vertices of one particle are pulled together/apart. Measured on the m21d as-built patches (every shape at weight 1):
   Breasts p99 22.7 mm / max 39.4 mm error, Throat p99 3.9, Mouth p99 2.6; the Ass avoided it only by NOT reducing (6 334 particles, exact welds). The fix is in `magica_gen.build_virtual`:
   `shape_tol` {shape: tolerance m}, `rot_aware` (predicted error includes the particle frame's rotation under the shape: `(mean delta - D_i) + (R1 R0^T - I)(P_i - centre)`),
   `vec_tol` (open/push directions), `bone_w_tol` (skinning weights), `label` (border/core never merge), `tol_scale` (per vertex), plus `max_tri_valence 15`, `max_normal_deg 60`;
   and `child_weights(shapes_r, shape_tol)` (a render vertex binds only to particles whose shape deltas agree with its own: otherwise a mask border drags it 43 mm).
   In `own_cloth.build` these come through `virtual_opts` (`shape_tol_mm` = {"*": mm, "<shape prefix>": mm}, longest prefix wins; `region_tol_scale`), `verify=True` adds
   `report["fidelity"]` (`patch_verify.py`: `shape_error`, `pose_error` with the runtime's own frame convention; rest error 1e-15 m).
   Tolerances (mm): Vagina/Anus/Muscles 1.0, InnerMesh 3.0 (hidden under garments), Mask_* 4.0, Robotic_/Default_ 3.0, face keys 0.6, vec 0.3, bone weights 0.12, reduce length only a cap (8 mm).
   Profile T (`patches/cloth_profile_T.json`): Mouth/Ass x1.2, Throat x1.5 -> 4 323 particles, 29 174 constraints (tifa 4 363 / 31 198), error: vulva (Vagina shape) mean 0.4, p99 1.5 mm; breast shapes <= 0.6 mm p99 visible, 3.3 mm hidden.
4. Shipped density (struct edge median, tifa/succubus): Breasts ~9.5 mm, Mouth 3.4-3.5, Ass 3.1-4.4 with p95 21-23 mm (variable resolution), Throat 10-13 mm; profile T lands on the same numbers.
Pitfalls: a uniform reduce looks fine at rest (child binding is exact at rest) and breaks only under shapes/garments; do not loosen Mask_* above 4 mm (a few hundred rotating vertices show
10 mm in every variant, that is the baseline); `Fixed` border particles must not absorb core vertices (`label`); always run `verify=True` and read the per-class table before accepting a profile.
Not done here: dropping shape channels (241 on the m21d patches) = per-frame managed blend-shape sync loops (cost_model 3.2), owner integrator/skinning.


